<HR><A NAME="seats"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Which Seats fit the Tipo?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
The standard seats in the 16V Tipo are pretty useless. Not only do you feel like
you are sitting on the car rather than inside it, the latteral support is
also non existant. Does anybody know where I can get a pair of the Recaro
front seats that were an option on the 5 door version and
also on left hand drive versions of the 3 door?

<BR><BR>

Does anyone know if the seats can be made lower? I don't think this is
possible as there is a huge crossmember right underneath the seat.
In the racing Tipo this has been cut away to enable the seat to rest on
the floor - I don't recommend doing this to your road car as the racing
Tipo has a extensive roll cage to provide the necessary structural
strength.
<P>
<A HREF="../owners/richard_bower.html">Richard Bower</A> says 
that the height of the driver's seat is crazy. When he test drove the
car he figured that it must be stuck on the high setting. Only when he
gave it a good look over at home did he find that it was already at the
lowest. On previous cars He's been able to lower the seats a little by
removing the rubber spacers under the runner. The Tipo seems to have the
seats mounted without any washer. Normally the washer is there to
eliminate vibration, how can the Tipo manage without?
<P>
Richard thought about remanufacturing an new mount for the runner. He
think this would at least lower the rear of the seat and improve things
a bit.

<BR><BR>

I've spoken to Fiat UK about the optional Recaro seats. Apparently they
were pad based "modular" type. This means that to buy them now I would
need to buy leg pads, seat back, etc seperately. Even if I could track
them down, they would cost at least £600 per seat!

<BR><BR>

An alternative would be aftermarket seats from say Cobra, Recaro or Corbeau.
Apparently Alfa seats will also fit. Has anybody experience of either of
these? My car is a 3 door version so the seat would have to tilt forward to
allow access to the rear seats of the car. The original 3 door seats also
slide forward, is this possible with aftermarket seat frames?
<BR><BR>

I have recently attended the British Motor Show in Birmingham and spoke to
a Recaro representative. They do not produce mounting frames for the Tipo,
these would have to be custom made at a cost of about &pound;100 per seat.
<BR><BR>

<A HREF="../owners/alex_neagu.html">Alex Neagu</A> says that the
optional Recaro seats are not bucket seats and the shape is almost the
same as the standard FIAT items. There is not much support at the shoulders
and are simply orthopedic seats. He has seen better seats for much
less money.

<HR><A NAME="wheels"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">What wheel/tyre combinations do you have?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
What is the biggest wheel and tyre size you have got working without any rubbing
or reshaping underneath the wheel arches? Was this with the car lowered as well?
The original wheels are 6&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;15 with 185/55R15 tyres.
I have fitted 195/50R15 tyres on my car such that
they are 10mm wider but still maintain roughly the same outer circumference (they
actually have a 1.5% smaller diameter).
<BR><BR>
Based on the standard Tipo 16V tyre size of 185/55R15 the following table
illustrates alternative wheel and tyre combinations
whilst still maintaining roughly the same overall diameter.
Yokohama say that a tolerance of plus or
minus 5% in diameter is permissible. I have however, kept this table
within 2% as this is enough to provide an ample choice without the
effects being noticable. The figures
in brackets indicate the actual total diameter in mm.<BR><BR>

<TABLE BORDER="1" BGCOLOR="#333333" CELLPADDING="5">
<TR>
<TD>&nbsp;</TD>
<TD><CENTER><B>-2%</B></CENTER></TD>
<TD><CENTER><B>-1%</B></CENTER></TD>
<TD><CENTER><B>0%</B></CENTER></TD>
<TD><CENTER><B>+1%</B></CENTER></TD>
<TD><CENTER><B>+2%</B></CENTER></TD>
</TR>

