<FONT COLOR="#ff0000"><SUP>&#134;</SUP></FONT>
Included for completeness but highly likely to be far too wide
to fit on a Tipo 16V.<BR>

<FONT COLOR="#00ff00"><SUP>&#135;</SUP></FONT>
These are known to fit the Tipo 16V even if it has been lowered 35mm.


<BR><BR><BR>

<DL>
<DT>
The total diameters shown above can be calculated using:<BR><BR>
<DD>
15&quot; diameter rims are equal to 381mm (15&quot; &times; 25.4)<BR><BR>

A 185/55 tyre has a side wall height of 102mm (185mm &times; 55%)<BR><BR>

Total diameter of 15&quot; rim with a 185/55 tyre is 585mm (381 + 102 &times; 2)<BR><BR>
</DL>

If a wheel and tyre combination has a larger diameter than standard
then the speedometer will underread. If the wheel and tyre combination
has a smaller diameter then the speedometer will overread.
For example if the overall diamter was 2% larger and the speedometer was
reading 100 mph then we would actually be travelling 102 mph. I think
this is an exceptable tolerance.
<BR><BR>

I was originally going to fit 7&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;17 rims with 205/40R17
tyres. Unfortunatly a set of wheels and tyres start to get expensive &#126;
&pound;1000 at these sizes. Fitting 17&quot; wheels though
would
<A HREF="wheels.html">look</A>.
fantastic. The ride would be much firmer and more stable with the
combination of slightly more rubber in contact with the road,
wider rims and narrower sidewalls. 
However, having spoken to David Moore at
<A HREF="http://www.tuningshop.co.uk" target="_blank">The Tuning WebShop</A>
I changed my mind. He said:<BR><BR>

<DL>
<DT>
<DD>
<I>
17&quot; wheels are great for shows and big brake conversions. Grip
will be vastly increased and give the car kart style handling.
However break-away will be dramatically faster
because lower profile tyres required (205/40R17) give less warning when
losing traction.
On very bumpy surfaces, they will skip which is a little scary. Tramlining
is normal on all cars with wide, low profile tyred cars.<BR><BR>

16&quot; wheels are better if you want a nice &quot;chuckable&quot;
car that you can slide around bends, balancing grip on the throttle.
He recommends 215/40R16 tyres provided they will fit under the wheel
arches.
</I>
</DL>

<A HREF="../owners/angelo_orlando.html">Angelo Orlando</A> says
that in Italy it is illegal to use a wheel and tyre combination that is
different from the manufacturers approved sizes. Therefore for the 16V Tipo
they are limited to 185/55R15 or 195/50R15 on 6&times;15 rims. Angelo also
says that he prefers MICHELIN PILOT GT-SX tyres as an optimal compromise
between sport and longevity. After 20,000km they are still in good
condition.
<BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/lutfi_okay.html">Lutfi Okay</A> says
that he has used UNIROYAL 195/55 R15 tyres for the last couple of
months and cliams they perform excellently.
<BR><BR>
Calvin Jones says
that he uses Pirelli P5000 195s as they last quite well, 
are reasonably grippy and they cost under &pound;50 each
<BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/steve_whiteside.html">Steve Whiteside</A> says
that the standard FIAT wheels cost &pound;220 each. He tried to fit a
pair of Fondmetal 1600 alloys however they would not fit over the
cars standard brake calipers.
<BR><BR>
Nareman Virk
advises against going for 17&quot; wheels as they would
adversely effect the road holding. Instead he changed the standard
wheels to a set of 15&quot; Momo Extremes. Nareman originally used Pirelli P6000
but did not like them, he then changed to Yokohama A520s. These had good dry grip
but wore out quickly.He's currently using
195/50 Goodyear Eagle F1s for which he has nothing but praise. Good grip in dry
and wet, good wear and a sexy tread pattern.

<BR>


<HR><A NAME="tyre_wear"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">How long do your tyres last?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/matt_stafford.html">Matt Stafford</A> says
that even without being driven hard the Tipo is heavy on tyres. How long
do you find your tyres last?


<HR><A NAME="locks"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Can anyone explain de-locking?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
I am confused as to how delocking is intended to work. I can understand that
the car must have a remote lock/unlock feature combined with central
locking. When the button on the remote is pressed the car is unlocked and
the handle is simply used to open the door. If this is further combined
with central locking then all doors unlocked and can be opened from outside.
<BR><BR>
When a key is turned in a standard lock it turns a barrel behind
the lock. This is connected to a rod that is attached to the lock
mechanism. The circular action of the barrel rotating is converting into a
linear action and used to unlock/lock the mechanism it is attached to. If
central locking is fitted the connecting rod also makes an electrical
contact that signals the solenoids attached to the locking mechanism do the
locking/unlocking on each door they are attached to.
<BR><BR>
When the car is delocked the keyhole, barrel and connecting
rod are removed. The signal to the solenoid to lock/unlock the car now
comes solely from the remote locking electronics. Since the barrel is removed it
is impossible for thiefs to jam a screwdriver into the keyhole in an
attempt to turn the barrel behind. It is also impossible to slide something
down inside the door to push/pull the rod as it has also been removed. The
car is therefore far more secure (providing the remote locking electronics
are).
<BR><BR>
If the above is correct what happens if the car has a flat battery? If you
are using electronic solenoids to unlock the doors how will they unlock?
One solution might be to leave the boot lock in place. You could then use
the key in this lock and climb through. Okay if you have a hatch back but
not if you have a saloon. This would also be a potential security risk
since if the thief manages to turn the lock in boot, say with a
screwdriver, then as all locks are connected via the central locking all
the doors will unlock.
<BR><BR>
A second problem would be if I want to remove the battery from the car to
charge it. I live on the second floor of an apartment and there is no cover
or power point near the car. How do I lock the car after the battery has
been removed? Not to mention how I would unlock it again?
<BR><BR>
The next stage would be to remove the door handles. As well as
looking very smart it could help prevent car jacking in certain situations
as the car jacker would have nothing to grab in order to open the doors. I
assume that this is performed by another solenoid doing the same job as the
original door handle. This would pop the door open a touch so that it can
be opened completely. Does this mean that all doors will spring open when
the car is unlocked? Once the driver is inside would they then have to pull
to the passenger door shut? If only the drivers door opens the central
locking is pointless since if a passenger wanted to get in the driver would
have to lean over and open the door for them.
<BR>

