<HR><A NAME="mirrors"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Aftermarket Wing Mirrors?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
Does anyone know of a stylish alternative to the standard FIAT Tipo wing
mirrors? Bear in mind that they should ideally be electronically adjustable
and heated.

<HR><A NAME="powerloss"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Losing power when the engine is hot?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/john_albertsen.html">John Albertsen's</A> Tipo is losing about 10 - 16 bhp at around 5,500rpm
when the engine is warm, &#126;85<SUP>o</SUP>c. Has anyone any suggestions
or previous experience with this?
<BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/angelo_orlando.html">Angelo Orlando</A> says 
that the loss of power from a hot engine could be caused by several things.
The timing of the engine is critical especially on the 16V. The catalytic
converter may be blocked. The fuel mixture may also be wrong but this can be
caused by many factors. If the engine is running hot it's normal for it
to lose power because when the metal in the engine expands it will
lose compression. Lack of compression can also be the result of wear and
tear caused by high mileage.
One way to solve the problem might be to change the water pump to
dissipate the heat of the engine quicker.

<HR><A NAME="timing"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Why do the 3 and 5 door 0-60 mph times differ so much?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
Acording to the offical FIAT brouchure the 5 door FIAT Tipo 16v reaches
60 mph in 7.9 seconds however the 3 door takes
9.8 seconds. Can anybody explain why there is such a difference?
<BR><BR>
Both cars
have a catalytic converter. The 5 door produces 148 bhp at 6250 rpm and
131 lbs ft of torque at 5000 rpm where as the 3 door produces 142 bhp at 6000
rpm and 135 lbs ft of torque at 4500 rpm. I'm not sure of the cars weights nor
whether the gear ratios were changed between versions. Maybe the 3 door
version required a change into 3rd gear for the tests as the old test was
0 to 60 mph where as now it is 0 to 100kmh (62 mph). For the 3 door model
FIAT added extra strengthening to the shell which might have increased
it's weight. FIAT also detuned the engine in the 3 door for more torque
but basically it's the same engine. The rebuild that was recently
performed on my engine was also carried out on my friends 5 door
engine. The garage reported no real differences in design between the two
engines.
<BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/andy_giles.html">Andy Giles'</A>
racing Tipo with 179 bhp and 1110 kg of weight has been
acuractly timed at 7.18 seconds for 0-60 mph.
Mike Jukes says that with
his 3 door he gets sub 7.5 second 0-60 mph times.

<HR><A NAME="oil"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">What oil consumption figures do you get?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
What oil and petrol consumption figures do you get. Is there any particular
oil that you find works best?
<A HREF="../owners/david_gallone.html">David Gallone's</A> Tipo gets though 1 litre of oil for
every 1000 km and
<A HREF="../owners/ben_sleeman.html">Ben Sleeman's</A> Tipo gets
though 1/2 litre of oil for every 1000 miles. Is this normal?
<P>
<A HREF="../owners/angelo_orlando.html">Angelo Orlando</A> says &quot;yes&quot;.
Infact anything between 1 litre for 1000-1200 Km is normal 
for a 2000cc engine made by Lancia. Think yourselves luckly
that you don't own a Lancia Delta Integrale which drinks oil at the alarming rate of
1 litre for 500-700 Km. I wouldn't say no to one though!!!
Angelo also says that in general a 15W50 oil during the summer and a 10W50
oil in the winter works best. He uses Motul.

<P>
<A HREF="../owners/karim_kassir.html">Karim Kassir</A> says that
the Tipo 16V can consume about 0.9 litres every 1000 km. he's noticed that
it really depends on the type of oil and on the revs of the engine.
While driving on motorways the car consumes more oil due to the high revs
of the engine. Using expensive synthetic oil only increases the oil
consumption. He currently use MOBIL 10W-40 motor oil.

<P>
If your car uses a lot of oil check out this
<A HREF="http://www.qsresearch.com/qstate/techbull/engine~1.htm" target="_blank">web site page</A> for where it
might be going.

