</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/ben_sleeman.html">Ben Sleeman</A>
would like to know if there is an equivalent of a Haynes workshop manual
for the Tipo 16V. I
have seen a Haynes manual for a 1.6 but that's not much good for us.
<P>
<A HREF="../owners/karim_kassir.html">Karim Kassir</A> says that
there is a detailed repair manual for the Fiat Tipo (all series including
the 16V), but it's in German. If you want one the ISBN
number is: ISB N 3-7688-0870-X. Karim says there is also a company in
Germany which sells workshop manuals for FIAT.
He has not called them yet so he doesn't know if they also have Tipo 16V
manuals. The phone number is : +49&nbsp;-&nbsp;521&nbsp;-&nbsp;403956.
<P>
Until we can find a manual, the most helpful action we can take would
be for those who
have to carry out repairs on their car to email me what they had to do
such that I can document it for other owners.

<HR><A NAME="steering"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Does anyone know why the steering may become very light?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
Over the last couple  weeks the steering on <A HREF="../owners/tony_ghirardi.html">Tony Ghirardi's</A>
Tipo has become very light and occasionally he gets a &quote;clunk&quote;
(Very technical term that!) when dropping off the accelerator. Does
anyone have any ideas on what could be causing this. The two may not be
related, but they seem to have appeared at around the same time.

<HR><A NAME="sixspeedgearbox"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Does anyone know of a 6 speed gearbox for the Tipo?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
I don't know of any 6 speed gearboxes available for the Tipo 16V.
The Limited Edition Fiat Coupe 20V Turbo has a 6 speed gearbox but I don't
know whether it would fit the Tipo. In any case the 6th gear is just
an overdrive as Fiat have not changed the ratios of the other 5 gears. This
makes the gearbox good for cruising on motorways but it wouldn't help
with in gear accelleration. If you want better accelleration then maybe a
better option would be to fit the gearbox from a 1.8 Tipo which has lower
gearing but the potential top speed would be lower.

<HR><A NAME="headlights"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">What do you think of the Tipos headlights?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/giles_stewart.html">Giles Stewart</A>
lives out in the country and regularly drives along country roads at night.
He has found it a bit of a strain as he thinks the standard lights 60/55W are
a bit weak. He has fitted a couple of Hella driving lights and the
difference is remarkable. Has anyone else improved the Tipo lights?
<BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/lutfi_okay.html">Lutfi Okay</A>
says that the original lights were so weak that he changed the bulbs for
100W elements. Please note that the standard headlight wiring is not
designed to carry the amount of current these high wattage bulbs will draw.
Extra wiring and a heavy duty relay will need to be fitted.
<BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/karim_kassir.html">Karim Kassir</A>
says don't ever fit (illegal) 100W bulbs! Try measuring the voltage on
the cable just before the headlights. He read an article regarding the
Volkswagen Golf in which it was mentioned that only approximately 10.4
volts get through to the headlights. The rest is lost between battery,
cheap cables, on/off switches and so on. Try connecting the headlights
directly to the battery and use the original cables to the headlights
to supply an on/off switch. It should give the headlights definitely
more power than before, even with using only 55W bulbs.

<BR><BR>
In the UK unless your car was registered before 1986, you are limited
to fitting a bulb of the same wattage rating as was fitted at thte factory.
If you fit 100W bulbs you may leave yourself open to blame in the case
of an accident. If the other party claim they were blinded by your
bright headlamps, and the police spot they are illegally fitted you could
be in trouble. As ever, it's probably best to check with your
insurance company.
<BR><BR>
Please also be aware that higher wattage bulbs get extremely hot. If the
headlamps of your car regularly mist up inside then the water on the bulb will
probably cause it to blow.
<BR><BR>
On the subject of tinted bulbs/lenses, under the UK's Department of
Transport's Lighting Regulation of 1989, a headlamp bulb must produce
a white light. Some bulbs produce a bluish tint as a side effect of using
an electrical arc as the light source. This color is quite good to drive
behind, but is more likely to cause glare to oncoming drivers.
Glare is caused by the overstimulation of Retinal Purple in the human eye by
this very color range and hinders night vision for a long time after
a relatively brief exposure.
<BR><BR>
There is a very good
<A HREF="http://lighting.mbz.org" target="_blank">web site</A>
which has lots of FAQ about car lighting

<A HREF="tipo/faq/faq.html#ignition">

<HR><A NAME="clutch"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">What aftermarket clutches fit the Tipo 16V?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
Several people have asked me what aftermarket clutches fit the Tipo 16V. I'm
afraid I don't know. If anybody has any advice then please let me know.
As part of my engine rebuild my clutch has been replaced with a far
superior racing clutch. This is a four paddle type for high friction and is
extremely strong to handle the hike in power. I don't know the exact model
yet but it is very expensive at about &pound;300.

