<HR><A NAME="single_wiper"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Does anyone know how to convert the Tipo to a single wiper?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
Does anyone have any views on single wiper conversions for the Tipo.
I quite fancy the idea as they look extremely good especially when the
wiper parks vertically. I've only ever been in one car with a single wiper,
that was a Citroen. Whilst in the car I noticed the downside to having a
single wiper. When in use the blade wipes the rain to the bottom of
the windscreen rather than to the side. Therefore the wind blows the
water back up the windscreen between strokes making it difficult to see.
This side effect is not so much of a safety issue if the wiper parks at the
bottom of the drivers side rather than the passengers.
<P>
Ideas: 
<UL>
<LI> On the Tipo there is a plastic panel underneath the bonnet through
     which the wiper arms attach to the underlying mechanism. The
     passenger side wiper arm hole seems to be pretty much in the center
     of the car which is a good start. The drivers side hole could be
     covered in a blank although it's not vital as they are both hidden
     under the bonnet. Also because they are hidden under the bonnet it
     would not be necessary to buy a single wiper version of the
     plastic panel. This plastic panel is also not specially shaped
     for twin wipers and shouldn't restrict the larger arc that would
     be needed. 

<LI> Does anyone have any mechanical knowledge on how the wiper mechanism
     works and how it attaches to the car? 

<LI> Would it be possible to fit a single wiper mechanism off
     another car such as the FIAT Uno or Citreon range. 

<LI> Would single wiper conversions for say a VW Golf or Ford escort
     fit the Tipo? 

<LI> Is it possible to modify the throw of the existing mechanism by
     altering the leverage point or would this put too much strain
     on the wiper motor? 

<LI> How does the wiper park mechanism work? Would it be possible to
     get the wiper to park vertically in the middle of the
     windscreen rather than on the passenger side? Can the mechanism be
     altered to sweep left then right rather than left only or right only
     without the need for say a custom relay.

<LI> Is parking in the middle of the windscreen legal? 

<LI> What's the legal minimum sweep area for the MOT? 

<LI> Would anyone else be interested in a conversion if we managed
     to come up with something. Pool resources ideas etc. 

<LI> The conversion would probably need a longer arm and blade, is
     the spline in the Tipo a standard size in order to fit non
     Tipo arms or would the Tipo arm have to be modified. 

<LI> Due to the shape of the windscreen and the location of the wiper
     spine the wiper would not cover the whole height of the windscreen.
     Have a look at the passenger wiper in motion and you will see what
     I mean. Even though when parked it touches the edge of the screen,
     at the highest point of it's stroke its still some distance off the
     top of the windscreen. The high nature of the Tipo seats make this
     more of a problem.

<LI> Is it worth it? 

<LI> Is it a good idea? 
</UL>

A simple single wiper conversion can be performed by removing the drivers
side wiper. Now set up the passengers wiper such that it parks at about
45 degrees rather than flat. Starting from this position it will sweep over
the drivers side just like a true single wiper would. However it will
annoy the hell out of the passengers having the wiper parked in thier face.
There are (at least) two problems with this however. The passenger wiper
movement is little more than 90 degrees and the arm tends to judder past
half way.


<HR><A NAME="low_oil_pressure"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">What might cause low oil pressure?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/karim_kassir.html">Karim Kassir</A> says that
if you have too low an oil pressure then check the quality of the oil!
If it's really black (even after only 6000 - 8000 km) then it's time to
change it. This gets the oil pressure back to normal values.

<HR><A NAME="drivetrain_jerkiness"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Would a chip help with jerkiness of car?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/stefan_starkie.html">Stefan Starkie</A>
asks if he got his Tipo chipped, would it sort out some of the
drivetrain jerkiness it suffers from at low speeds?

<HR><A NAME="fuel_type"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Which fuel should I use in my Tipo 16V?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/gunay_kaya.html">Gunay Kaya</A>
asks whether he should use 95 or 98 octane fuel in his Tipo. I think that
the car will run quite happily on either. 98 octane will give you more power
and is a better quality fuel and hence less likely to cause knocking.
However the price of 98 (Super-unleaded) octane fuel is very expensive
compared to standard 95 (Unleaded) fuel here in the UK.

<HR><A NAME="spare_parts"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Where can I get spare parts from?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
For most of us it's not too difficult to find spare parts for the Tipo 16V.
Even though the Tipo was discontinued in October 1995 you can still
find most parts direct from FIAT dealers. Many parts are also shared
by other models. Second hand parts are more difficult as finding
a Tipo 16V in a scrap yard is very difficult. Come to think of it,
finding any Tipo in a scrap yard is difficult.
However 
<A HREF="../owners/james_byam.html">James Byam</A> has a unique problem.
He lives in the West Indies and is having great difficulty finding a
dealer he can rely on. Can anyone recommend a parts distributer that can
supply anywhere in the world?

<HR><A NAME="cd_rom"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Where can I get a FIAT parts CD ROM from?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/andres_campo.html">Andr&eacute;s Oscar del Campo</A>
would like to know where he can get a copy of the FIAT CDROM. This
should contain the part numbers and diagrams for the Tipo 16V as well
as a price guide.

<HR><A NAME="oil_filler"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Why did the oil filler cap come off?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
When examining his car
<A HREF="../owners/nigel_gladstone.html">Nigel Gladstone</A>
discovered that his
oil filler cap had come out. What does this mean? Does it indicate simply
that it was not previously put on properly, worn
filler cap rubber, worn piston rings, a blockage in the oil ways
or too much thrashing of the car. The oil pressure does not seem too
high.


<HR><A NAME="oil_filter_leak"><B><FONT COLOR="#3366ff">Why is oil leaking from the pipes near the oil filter?
</FONT></B></A><BR><BR>
<A HREF="../owners/steve_whiteside.html">Steve Whiteside</A> says
his car appears to be leaking oil from two rubber pipes which
exit from the base of the oil filter. They only drip at the moment but is
this a serious problem? Is there an underlying oil block building up
pressure somewhere thus blowing oil out of the rubber hose connections
which appear to be tight?

